Log Book Entry — Section 27: Dark Matter Close Encounters & Collision Tracking  
Author: Abhinav Vatsa  
Date: March 2026  
Duration: ~10.5 hours  

---

### Objective  
Extend the MW–M31 merger analysis pipeline to quantify dark matter close encounters as a proxy for interaction dynamics in a collisionless N-body system. The aim was to extract encounter-driven statistics (rates, kinematics, energy exchange, relaxation) while remaining consistent with the physical assumption that dark matter does not undergo literal collisions.

---

### Development Notes  
- Initial code template generated using Claude, then significantly modified, extended, and integrated into the existing pipeline  
- Substantial effort spent on:
  - Adapting template structure to match pipeline globals and data flow  
  - Ensuring physical consistency of derived quantities  
  - Optimizing performance and memory usage  
  - Building full visualization and animation suite  

---

### Conceptual Framework  
Close encounters defined via threshold distance ( d_{\text{enc}} = 1 ,\text{kpc} ), representing gravitationally significant interactions. These encode:

- Two-body scattering and dynamical friction analogues  
- Local density enhancements without radial binning  
- Relative velocity regimes (cold vs. hot encounters)  
- Time-dependent interaction rate ( \Gamma(t) \sim n^2 \sigma v_{\text{rel}} )

---

### Methodology  

1. Encounter Detection  
- Implemented KD-tree radius search (scipy.spatial.cKDTree)  
- Subsampled ( N_{\text{probe}} = 5000 ) particles per snapshot  
- For each encounter computed:
  - Separation ( d )  
  - Relative velocity ( v_{\text{rel}} )  
  - Deflection angle ( \theta )  
  - Energy transfer ( |\Delta E| )  
  - Encounter midpoint  

2. Performance Strategy  
- Avoided ( \mathcal{O}(N^2) ) scaling  
- Combined statistical sampling with exact neighbor queries  
- Achieved efficient runtime (~1–2 s per snapshot)

3. Derived Diagnostics  
- Total encounter rate ( \Gamma(t) )  
- Radial encounter profile ( \Gamma(r,t) )  
- Velocity distribution ( P(v_{\text{rel}}) )  
- Cold encounter fraction (( v_{\text{rel}} < 50 ,\text{km/s} ))  
- Mean deflection angle and energy transfer  
- Two-body relaxation timescale ( t_{\text{relax}}(r) )

4. Relaxation Analysis  
Used:
[
t_{\text{relax}} = \frac{0.34 \sigma^3}{G^2 m \rho \ln \Lambda}
]
to quantify numerical relaxation effects across radial shells.

